\section{Functional \code{a} is not Cockerham \code{alpha} once pairs exist}{

\code{ad_terms(coding = "cockerham")} takes the average effect \code{alpha}, not the
functional \code{a}. Already without pairs \verb{alpha = a + (q - p) d}; with pairs it
also needs \verb{sum_l e_jl (2 p_l - 1)}, which \code{ad_terms()} never sees. Feeding
functional \code{a} to the Cockerham branch therefore changes the \strong{genotypic
values}, not just how the variance is split between components. For a
hand-written model with pairs, use functional coding for both builders.
